Pebble's tableCacheShard.Close() intentionally keeps its releaseLoop
goroutine alive when it detects leaked iterators. This prevents
testing/synctest from detecting quiescence, causing deadlock in tests
that run Pebble under a synctest bubble.

This patch makes tableCacheShard.Close() always close releasingCh so the
goroutine exits, even when iterators were leaked. Acceptable tradeoff
because on v23-ratel we run inproc test clusters under synctest, and
synctest quiescence is more valuable than the leaked-iterator diagnostic
that this branch was preserving.

To apply: requires a pebble fork. Not wired up yet — when we ship
synctest chaos tests, add `replace github.com/cockroachdb/pebble =>
github.com/semistrict/pebble v1.1.5-ratel1` to go.mod pointing at a
branch with this change applied.

--- a/table_cache.go
+++ b/table_cache.go
@@ -1051,17 +1051,9 @@
 	c.mu.handCold = nil
 	c.mu.handTest = nil

-	// Only shutdown the releasing goroutine if there were no leaked
-	// iterators. If there were leaked iterators, we leave the goroutine running
-	// and the releasingCh open so that a subsequent iterator close can
-	// complete. This behavior is used by iterator leak tests. Leaking the
-	// goroutine for these tests is less bad not closing the iterator which
-	// triggers other warnings about block cache handles not being released.
-	if err != nil {
-		c.releasing.Wait()
-		return err
-	}
-
+	// Always shutdown the releasing goroutine, even if there are leaked
+	// iterators. Leaving it running prevents synctest from detecting
+	// quiescence.
 	close(c.releasingCh)
 	c.releasing.Wait()
 	c.releaseLoopExit.Wait()
